## 🔴 Which skill to run, and when

Skills are invoked by *judgment*, not by a trigger — nothing fires them automatically, and nothing
warns you when one was skipped. **This table is the rule.** Treat it as binding, and say in your
report which skills you ran.

| Before you… | Run |
|---|---|
| start a session | `start-session` |
| plan a fix or feature | `plan` — then the other agent runs `plan-review` |
| **write or edit any production code** | 🔴 **`coder`** — every time, no exception |
| touch a screen, component, state or theme | `frontend` (with `coder`) |
| touch a route, handler, service or data access | `backend` (with `coder`) |
| touch a table, column, index or migration | `schema` |
| touch personal data, a new dependency, or an external service | `compliance` — **before** writing, not after |
| touch a write path that changes state | `audit-trail` |
| say a change is done | `local-testing` |
| review a diff | `codereview` |
| review UI work | `design-review` (with `codereview`) |
| audit the whole repository for security | `complete-security-review` |
| end a session | `end-session` |

**The two that are hardest to remember and cost the most when skipped:**

- 🔴 **`coder` runs for every piece of production code.** Not just for big changes. It carries the
  twin protocol and the Red→Green discipline, and a one-line fix is exactly where those get skipped.
- 🔴 **`compliance` runs *before* the code, not after.** It is a gate, and a gate that runs afterwards
  is a report.

If you are unsure whether a skill applies, **run it and say it did not apply.** "I ran `compliance`
and found nothing" and "I did not run `compliance`" are different sentences, and only one of them is
a gate. Declaring a skill you skipped is honest; skipping it silently is how the discipline erodes
one small change at a time.

### Open the skill file every time — do not run it from memory

Running a skill means **opening its `SKILL.md` and reading it**, on that invocation, including the
tenth time you run it in a week. Being sure you remember the procedure is not evidence that the file
still says what you remember — the file gets specialized for this project, and it changes.

This failure leaves nothing behind to notice: an agent works from a half-remembered version, a step
quietly does not happen, and the gap surfaces later or never. It has been measured in another kit —
in [LAAW](https://github.com/vitoremanuellds/LAAW), a skill carried its own condensed copy of the
rules so agents could skip the reread, and an agent invented a status value that did not exist in the
closed list, because what it worked from was a summary rather than the rule. The shortcut was
deleted.

Same discipline as `{{RECORDS_DIR}}/info.md` above, for the same reason.

### Delegated executors do not choose

`agy` and `copilot` have no skill system. They read the skill files you name in the `skills`
parameter of the MCP call — so **you** pick, explicitly, on every delegated task: `["coder"]` plus
`frontend` or `backend`. Never `design-review` or `local-testing`: they need a browser and a terminal
the executor does not have.

