## Non-negotiable rule: SDD + BDD + TDD

No production code is written without spec (SDD) → behavior (BDD) → red test (TDD). No exceptions, not
even for a small change.

1. **SDD — the spec lives at the top of the test file**: what the contract is, **why it exists** (the
   bug or the decision that originated it) and what is business rule. Model: `<exemplary test file>`.
2. **BDD — behavior, not implementation**: `Test<BusinessScenario>` → `should_<result>_when_<condition>`,
   in the language of the operation.
3. **TDD — Red → Green → Refactor**: write the test, run it and **see it fail**; only then write the
   minimum to pass.

### What to test (by priority)

| Priority | Target | Why |
|---|---|---|
| 🔴 High | Integrity of `{{CRITICAL_ASSET}}` | irreversible damage |
| 🔴 High | Extracted pure logic | it is what can genuinely be tested |
| 🔴 High | The error path | does the message REACH the user? |
| 🟡 Medium | Rules extracted from the edge | |
| 🟢 Low | Widget/screen | needs a human eye |

**Mocks for the external only.** Use a real test file/database instead of mocking the I/O layer.

⚠️ **Green is not enough.** The suite proves the contract, not the product — after green, **exercise
it** (see `.claude/skills/local-testing/`).

